                <pb facs="0665" n="631" xml:id="pg631"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>In a statement issued to the press on October 11, 1947, the Department of State
                    took note of the end of proceedings of the Austrian Treaty Commission, reviewed
                    briefly the establishment, terms of reference, and activities of the Commission,
                    and concluded with an expression of regret over the undue delay in reaching
                    agreement on the outstanding points of difference in the Austrian treaty. The
                    statement also reaffirmed the hope of the United States of obtaining an Austrian
                    treaty at the earliest possible date on the basis of the work done by the
                    Commission and the intention of the United States to make every effort at the
                    forthcoming meeting of the Council of Foreign Ministers in London to resolve the
                    remaining outstanding issues regarding the treaty. For the text of the
                    statement, see Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>,
                    October 19, 1947, page 767.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
